    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d576">

                <pb facs="0644" n="532" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg532"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.50/68: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Vladivostok</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Macgowan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vladivostok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-19T11:00:00+08:47"><hi rendition="#italic">October 19,
                                1919, 11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 5:19
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>555. …</p>
                <p>I had opportunity yesterday to confer with all representatives of the United
                    States here and all agreed on the following points.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Political conditions eastern Siberia quieter than for months, possibly a
                        year.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>There is no danger whatever of Cossacks now attacking Americans or seizing
                        Chinese Eastern railway.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Anti-American propaganda has been decreased for some time and there is
                        evidence of more critical attention of Japanese [<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >sic</hi>].</item>
                    <pb facs="0645" n="533" xml:id="pg533"/>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Organized opposition and plotting against Kolchak is no longer heard of. I attribute 4 in large
                        measure to the Department telegrams Oct. 2, 6 p.m.<anchor n="78"
                            xml:id="d576-f1"/> and Oct. 8, 6 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">October
                            7, 2 p.m.?</hi>].<anchor n="79" xml:id="d576-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>If I may venture an opinion, the foregoing and favorable news from all fronts
                    afford an opportunity for the United States to express in action its sympathy
                    and support of a united, democratic Russia represented by Kolchak. His failure might result in break up
                    Russia into military states under foreign domination and permanent occupation
                    eastern Siberia by Japan which may be nearer <hi rendition="#italic">fait
                        accompli</hi> than is supposed . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Macgowan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
